    reading_text: `<h2>The World's Most Mysterious Manuscript</h2>
    <p>Housed inside the rare book library at Yale University sits a medieval volume that no human mind has ever managed to read: the Voynich Manuscript. Discovered in 1912 by an antique book dealer named Wilfrid Voynich, the handwritten book has baffled cryptographers, historians, and linguists for over a century. [A] It features an elegant, flowing script that matches no known language or alphabet system in human history.</p>
    <p>The visual contents of the manuscript are just as puzzling as the text itself. The pages are filled with colorful, detailed illustrations of bizarre plants that do not exist on Earth, strange astrological diagrams, and drawings of women bathing in interconnected tubes of green liquid. [B] Because of these unique images, early researchers assumed the volume was an ancient botanical manual or a secret textbook on medieval alchemy.</p>
    <p>During the Second World War, top military codebreakers, who had successfully decoded complex enemy military encryptions, attempted to crack the Voynich script. [C] Every mathematical algorithm and decryption technique failed completely, leading some experts to declare that the manuscript was a highly sophisticated hoax, written with meaningless characters to trick wealthy antique collectors.</p>
    <p>However, recent computer analysis conducted by artificial intelligence has challenged the hoax theory. AI algorithms examined the statistical patterns of the text, looking at word lengths and letter frequencies. [D] This indicates that the script is not random gibberish, but a real language or a highly complex cipher that follows authentic linguistic rules, even if we cannot translate its actual meaning yet.</p>
    <p>Carbon dating has officially placed the creation of the vellum pages between 1404 and 1438, confirming its authenticity as a genuine medieval artifact. Until someone discovers a key or a translation matrix, the Voynich Manuscript will remain an impenetrable enigma, guarding its secrets from the modern world.</p>`,
