    reading_text: `<h2>Preserving the Starry Night</h2>
    <p>For most of human history, looking up at the night sky offered a breathtaking view of thousands of stars, constellations, and the vibrant band of the Milky Way. Today, however, that universal human experience is rapidly disappearing. Due to the exponential expansion of urban centers, over eighty percent of the world's population lives under skies polluted by artificial light. [A] This phenomenon, known as light pollution, has created a permanent artificial twilight over our cities, completely erasing the stars.</p>
    <p>Light pollution is not merely an aesthetic loss for stargazers; it has severe ecological and biological consequences. Millions of years of evolution have programmed life on Earth to rely on a predictable cycle of light and dark. [B] Artificial lighting confuses migrating birds, who navigate by the stars, causing them to crash into illuminated skyscrapers. It also disrupts the reproduction cycles of nocturnal insects and marine animals like sea turtle hatchlings, who crawl away from the ocean toward bright coastal streetlights.</p>
    <p>Humans are not immune to these disruptions either. Continuous exposure to artificial blue light at night suppresses the production of melatonin, the hormone responsible for regulating sleep cycles. [C] This chronic sleep disruption has been linked by health professionals to increased rates of depression, obesity, and immune deficiencies among city dwellers.</p>
    <p>To combat this growing crisis, an environmental movement led by the International Dark-Sky Association is establishing 'Dark Sky Places' worldwide. These are protected parks, reserves, and communities that have committed to eliminating unnecessary light pollution. [D] This includes installing shielded outdoor lamps that direct light downward onto the ground rather than scattering it into the sky, and using warm amber LED bulbs instead of harsh blue-white lights.</p>
    <p>Preserving dark skies does not mean turning off all city lights and returning to the dark ages. It simply requires intelligent, efficient lighting design that respects the natural environment. By taking control of our urban glow, we can restore our connection to the cosmos, ensuring that future generations can still look up in wonder at the infinite beauty of a starry night.</p>`,
